<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!-- App extensions MUST run sandboxed (Apple requirement); the main app
         deliberately does not (src-tauri/entitlements.plist).

         This extension claims NO App Group. The bridge socket used to live in
         the shared container, which forced the non-sandboxed app to reach into
         ~/Library/Group Containers/ on every launch — and since macOS 15 that
         raises the kTCCServiceSystemPolicyAppData prompt ("would like to access
         data from other apps") with no entitlement to opt out. The socket now
         lives at ~/.hippius/finder.sock and this sandbox reaches it through the
         exceptions below. Google Drive's own Finder extension is built the same
         way: sandboxed, no app group, socket outside every container.

         TWO rules, not one. Connecting to a Unix-domain socket needs both the
         file node (file-read*/file-write*) and the socket operation itself
         (network-outbound); Apple's temporary-exception.files.* keys cover
         regular files but NOT sockets, so both are expressed in SBPL. Each is
         anchored to the socket FILENAME rather than granted blanket — a bare
         `(allow network-outbound)` would hand the extension the entire
         network, which is more than it has ever needed.

         The home portion is deliberately `^/.*/` and NOT `^/Users/[^/]+/`.
         An entitlement cannot interpolate the home directory, and both sides
         derive the path from the account's REAL home — which is not always
         under /Users: network/mobile homes mount elsewhere, NFSHomeDirectory
         can be relocated, and the sandbox may evaluate the Data-volume
         firmlink form (/System/Volumes/Data/Users/...). Anchoring on /Users
         would deny those accounts both rules, and the failure is silent: the
         extension loads, connect() never succeeds, and every right-click just
         renders "Open Hippius to share" forever. The old container path was
         home-agnostic, so that would have been a regression. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.temporary-exception.sbpl</key>
    <array>
        <string>(allow file-read* file-write* (regex #"^/.*/\.hippius/finder\.sock$"))</string>
        <string>(allow network-outbound (regex #"^/.*/\.hippius/finder\.sock$"))</string>
    </array>
</dict>
</plist>
